<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine Phaulkon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1647–1688</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine Phaulkon was a Greek adventurer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> and became the chief minister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> in the 1680s. He promoted close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which sent embassies and troops, arousing the hostility of Siamese nobles. As Narai lay dying in 1688 he was arrested and executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-phaulkon.jpg" alt="Constantine Phaulkon" width="320" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre-Joseph d'Orléans (1641-1698) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_Phaulkon_Portrait_Engraving.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
